ORDERED: 
1. Before the court will recognize an attorney as representing a party, the attorney 
must comply with Florida Rule of General Practice and Judicial Administration 2.505(e) by: 
a. Filing the initial pleading on behalf of a party; 
b. Obtaining an order permitting substitution of counsel; or  
c. Filing a written notice of appearance as counsel or co-counsel. 
2. From time to time, with the permission of the client, an attorney may arrange 
for another attorney to appear at a proceeding to represent a party. Such “coverage” attorney 
must file a notice of appearance as co-counsel before the court will recognize the attorney as 
representing that party. Fla. R. Gen. Prac. & Jud. Admin 2.505(e). 
3. If an attorney signs an initial pleading or notice of appearance on behalf of a firm, 
any attorney from that firm will be permitted to appear in that action without filing a separate 
notice of appearance.  
4. Attorneys in family or probate matters seeking to enter a limited appearance on 
behalf of a party must file, at the time the attorney enters an appearance, a notice of limited 
appearance pursuant to Florida Family Law Rule of Procedure 12.040, or Florida Probate Rule 
5.030, signed by the party, and must otherwise fully comply with Florida Family Law Rule of 
Procedure 12.040, or Florida Probate Rule 5.030, as well as Florida Rules of General Practice 
and Judicial Administration 2.505(e). “Limited Appearance” attorneys are considered attorney 
of record until they have filed the notice of completion of limited appearance required by Rule 
12.040(c)(1), or Florida Probate Rule 5.030. 
5. Attorneys wishing to terminate their limited appearance in a civil proceeding 
must file a Termination of Limited Appearance, and the use of the attached form (Appendix A) 

